                <h2>Case Opening: An Independent Site Seller's Friday Shock</h2>
<p>A cross-border e-commerce team in Shenzhen received an official Google email on Friday afternoon: their ad account was suspended due to "suspicious payment activity." Team lead Manager Wang immediately logged into the backend and found all ads had been paused, and it was the critical preheating period before a major promotion.</p>
<p>This is not an isolated case. Based on our operational experience, Google Ads account suspension is one of the most common account risk control issues for overseas businesses. Today, we'll use this real case to break down the complete process from problem discovery to successful appeal.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Problem Breakdown: Common Reasons for Account Suspension</h2>
<h3>1. Payment-Related Issues</h3>
<p>This is the most common reason for account bans, including:</p>
<ul>
<li>Credit card or payment method chargebacks</li>
<li>Shared payment methods between accounts</li>
<li>Large-sum top-ups in a short period triggering risk controls</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Judgment Tip</strong>: If the email mentions "suspicious payment activity" or "invalid billing information," you can basically confirm it's a payment issue.</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>2. Policy Violations</h3>
<p>Ad content violating Google advertising policies can also lead to account suspension:</p>
<ul>
<li>False advertising or misleading claims</li>
<li>Sensitive industries without whitelisting</li>
<li>Landing page content inconsistent with ad promises</li>
</ul>
<h3>3. Phishing or Security Issues</h3>
<p>When there are abnormal logins or signs of potential account hijacking, Google may proactively suspend to protect advertisers.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Action Plan: Manager Wang's 48-Hour Appeal Record</h2>
<h3>Step 1: Collect Account Information (2 hours)</h3>
<p>After logging into the backend, Manager Wang first recorded:</p>
<ul>
<li>Customer ID (CUSTOMER_ID)</li>
<li>MCC-level relationship of the suspended account</li>
<li>Recent ad delivery reports (especially rejected ads)</li>
<li>Payment record screenshots</li>
</ul>
<h3>Step 2: Diagnose Suspension Reason (1 hour)</h3>
<p>Through email keywords and backend prompts, Manager Wang confirmed it was a <strong>payment method triggering risk controls</strong> — last week, team members tried topping up with a new credit card and entered the wrong CVV three times in a row.</p>
<h3>Step 3: Prepare Appeal Materials (5 hours)</h3>
<p><strong>Required Material Checklist:</strong></p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Material Type</th>
<th>Specific Content</th>
<th>Notes</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Identity Proof</td>
<td>Business license or legal representative ID</td>
<td>Company seal required</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Account Authorization</td>
<td>Proof you have permission to manage the account</td>
<td>Signed by legal representative</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Payment Proof</td>
<td>Recent 3-month statements</td>
<td>Showing company information</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Problem Explanation</td>
<td>Detailed description of what triggered risk controls</td>
<td>More effective in English</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Rectification Commitment</td>
<td>Explain future preventive measures</td>
<td>Specific and actionable</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Manager Wang additionally prepared:<br />
- Independent site domain ownership proof<br />
- Company bank account information (consistent with Google backend registration)<br />
- Third-party payment platform (PayPal, PingPong, etc.) authentication screenshots</p>
<h3>Step 4: Submit Appeal (30 minutes)</h3>
<p>Submit through the official appeal portal, <strong>with the following key points</strong>:</p>
<ol>
<li>Select <strong>English</strong> as the appeal language — processing is usually faster</li>
<li>Do not submit repeatedly — multiple submissions cause queue backlog</li>
<li>Keep total attachment size under 10MB</li>
<li>Keep your email accessible — the review team may follow up with questions</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>Result: A Turnaround Within 48 Hours</h2>
<p>Manager Wang submitted the appeal Friday evening and received Google's response Monday morning: the account was restored, but new top-ups required verification. After the team completed a small verification payment, the account was fully unbanned.</p>
<p><strong>Key Timeline:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Friday 18:00</strong> - Account suspended, immediately diagnose the cause</li>
<li><strong>Friday 21:00</strong> - Submitted complete appeal materials</li>
<li><strong>Monday 09:30</strong> - Received unbanning notification</li>
<li><strong>Monday 11:00</strong> - Completed verification, ads back online</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Avoidance Guide: 3 Principles for Appeal Material Preparation</h2>
<p><strong>1. Be Truthful and Complete, Leave No Gaps</strong></p>
<p>The review team cross-validates multiple information sources, and any discrepancies may extend the review cycle.</p>
<p><strong>2. Take Initiative to Admit Mistakes, Maintain a Sincere Attitude</strong></p>
<p>Even if you believe the risk control was a false positive, it's recommended to first express understanding, then state the actual situation.</p>
<p><strong>3. Prevention Beats Cure</strong></p>
<p>In daily operations, maintain: single payment method, stable delivery budget, consistent account login environment.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Quick Self-Check List</h2>
<p>If you're currently experiencing account suspension, check these first:</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] What are the email keywords?</li>
<li>[ ] Were there any abnormal operations in the last 3 days?</li>
<li>[ ] Is the payment method shared with other accounts?</li>
<li>[ ] Is the business license information within validity period?</li>
<li>[ ] Has the account been inactive for a long period?</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<p>There's no universal template for account appeals, but there's a universal preparation logic. After preparing the above materials, it's recommended to back them up locally before submitting through official channels. If you're also experiencing account issues, feel free to describe the situation in the comments — we can help you determine the appeal direction.</p>
